DTEND:20261107T190000Z
LOCATION:IU CINEMA
GEO:39.168613;-86.517376
SUMMARY:Aanikoobijigan (Ancestor / Great-Grandparent / Great-Grandchild) 
 Film Screening
DESCRIPTION:"What's the difference between an archaeologist and a grave r
 obber?" an elder asks. "A degree\," he laughs. Aanikoobijigan examines th
 e ongoing struggle to repatriate indigenous ancestors' remains from museu
 ms and archives\, exposing the thin line between archaeology and grave ro
 bbery. With striking visuals and grounded indigenous perspectives\, Aanik
 oobijigan challenges institutional resistance to decolonization and affir
 ms that returning ancestors to the earth restores memory\, dignity\, and 
 balance\, reminding us that no community can be whole until their ancesto
 rs are finally laid to rest. The film's directors\, Adam and Zack Khalil\
 , were recently named among the cohort of Filmmaker Magazine's 25 New Fil
 m Faces to Watch. [80 min\; documentary\; English and Anishinaabemowin wi
 th English subtitles]The screening will be followed by a conversation bet
 weenIU Indianapolis professor Dr. Holly Cusack-McVeigh\, Anthropology and
  Museum Studies and Public Scholar of Collections and Community Curation\
 , who is also featured in the filmDr. Olof Olafardottir-Hamilton\, Assist
 ant Director\, Office of the Native American Graves Protection and Repatr
 iation Act\, Indiana UniversityWilliam Johnson\, Curator/Operations Manag
 er at the Saginaw Chippewa Tribe of Michigan's Ziibiwing Center of Anishi
 naabe Culture and Lifeways and Chairman of the Michigan Anishinaabek Cult
 ural Preservation &amp\; Repatriation Alliance (MACPRA)\, who is also fea
 tured in the filmLogan Pappenfort\, Peoria Tribe of Indians of Oklahoma a
 nd Director of Tribal Relations at the Illinois State MuseumBurgundy Flet
 cher\, Tribal Historic Preservation Officer for the Peoria Tribe of India
 ns of Oklahoma  Presented in partnership with the Center for Documentary 
 Research and Practice.
X-ALT-DESC;FMTTYPE=text/html:<p>  "What's the difference between an archa
 eologist and a grave robber?" an elder asks. "A degree\," he laughs. <i>A
 anikoobijigan</i> examines the ongoing struggle to repatriate indigenous 
 ancestors' remains from museums and archives\, exposing the thin line bet
 ween archaeology and grave robbery. With striking visuals and grounded in
 digenous perspectives\, <i>Aanikoobijigan</i> challenges institutional re
 sistance to decolonization and affirms that returning ancestors to the ea
 rth restores memory\, dignity\, and balance\, reminding us that no commun
 ity can be whole until their ancestors are finally laid to rest. The film
 's directors\, Adam and Zack Khalil\, were recently named among the cohor
 t of <i>Filmmaker Magazine</i>'s 25 New Film Faces to Watch. [80 min\; do
 cumentary\; English and Anishinaabemowin with English subtitles]</p><p><i
 ><strong>The screening will be followed by a conversation between</strong
 ></i></p><ul><li><i>IU Indianapolis professor <strong>Dr. Holly Cusack-Mc
 Veigh</strong>\, Anthropology and Museum Studies and Public Scholar of Co
 llections and Community Curation\, who is also featured in the film</i></
 li><li><i><strong>Dr. Olof Olafardottir-Hamilton</strong>\, Assistant Dir
 ector\, Office of the Native American Graves Protection and Repatriation 
 Act\, Indiana University</i></li><li><i><strong>William Johnson</strong>\
 , Curator/Operations Manager at the Saginaw Chippewa Tribe of Michigan's 
 Ziibiwing Center of Anishinaabe Culture and Lifeways and Chairman of the 
 Michigan Anishinaabek Cultural Preservation &amp\; Repatriation Alliance 
 (MACPRA)\, who is also featured in the film</i></li><li><i><strong>Logan 
 Pappenfort\, </strong>Peoria Tribe of Indians of Oklahoma and Director of
  Tribal Relations at the Illinois State Museum</i></li><li><i><strong>Bur
 gundy Fletcher</strong>\, Tribal Historic Preservation Officer for the Pe
 oria Tribe of Indians of Oklahoma</i></li></ul><p>  Presented in partners
 hip with the Center for Documentary Research and Practice.</p>
