LOCATION:INDIANA MEMORIAL UNION (UNION BUILDING)
GEO:39.167655;-86.523537
SUMMARY:Acoustic Colonialism: Between Attentive Listening and Indigenous 
 Interference
DESCRIPTION:This talk will offer an author's view on the key theoretical\
 , methodological\, and historical concepts that form part of his book\, A
 coustic Colonialism: Acts of Mapuche Interference (Duke UP\, 2025). Theor
 izations regarding what the author calls "acoustic colonialism"\, "the co
 lonial ear"\, and "indigenous interference" reverberate throughout his bo
 ok\, including the Mapuche notion of allkütun (attentive listening). Cá
 rcamo-Huechante elaborates on these concepts in the context of Ngülü Ma
 pu–or what is currently known as central and southern Chile\, part of t
 he broader Mapuche territory (Wallmapu).
X-ALT-DESC;FMTTYPE=text/html:<p>  This talk will offer an author's view o
 n the key theoretical\, methodological\, and historical concepts that for
 m part of his book\, <strong>Acoustic Colonialism: Acts of Mapuche Interf
 erence</strong> (Duke UP\, 2025). Theorizations regarding what the author
  calls "acoustic colonialism"\, "the colonial ear"\, and "indigenous inte
 rference" reverberate throughout his book\, including the Mapuche notion 
 of allkütun (attentive listening). Cárcamo-Huechante elaborates on thes
 e concepts in the context of Ngülü Mapu–or what is currently known as
  central and southern Chile\, part of the broader Mapuche territory (Wall
 mapu).</p>
UID:20261108T010000Z-2473200@events.iu.edu
